Z||000007||Fierochic88||06-29-2001||12:27 PM||||I have said hypothetical car.  Basically in same condition.  Totally stock.  I paid $50 for it and it ran great.  Needed a clutch and one break calliper.<P>If I had a $100 to spend this week (went through this right before Carlisle) this is what I did:<P>1.) Buy some acrylic engine enamel in Universal Black and paint all the black exterior trim and rocker panels, along with the sail panel frames and bumpers.  ($12.00 for four cans of paint, plenty to do the car)<P>2.) Buy 2 arrowheads and put them in the sail panels' supports ($10.00 each).<P>3.) Get some rug cleaner and clean the carpets ($3.00)<P>4.) Wipe off the interior with some Super Clean and a rag ($5.00)<P>5.) Go to the junkyard and get a pair of Speaker Seats ($10/pair).  Recover them so they stay nice.<P>Halfway there...<P>6.)  Buy 2 more cans of spray paint and touch up the removable parts of your engine ($6.00)<P>7.) Remove the headliner, get some carpet to recover it and do that as well (not to hard if you're patient).  ($19.00)<P>8.) Use leftover headliner carpet and recover your visors. <P>9.) Get Interior Paint and Dutchboy Clear Coat and repaint your interior trim after prepping it with Prep-Sol (Laquer Thinner softens the trim and don't sand or you'll lose the ridges!).  ($10.00)<P>10.)  Buy some soap and tire cleaner and give it a bath ($5.00)<P>After all this it should look like a million bucks!<P>My car went from looking like a $50.00 car to a $1500.00 car.<P><BR>Good Luck!<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/jenscar-sig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
